From f6d7dae7e433b7530acfea176284ce9081860242 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristopher Bautista <cristopher.abg@icloud.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 16:26:30 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(llama): persist and restore session KV cache

Long conversations spend most of their time re-prefilling history that has not changed. On
memory-bandwidth-bound hardware (Apple Silicon) that dominates time-to-first-token, and today a
relaunched app pays it in full: llama.cpp can save its KV cache, but nothing on LlamaClient or
Context exposes it, and llama_context is package-scoped so a consumer cannot reach it either.

Adds LlamaClient.saveState(to:) / loadState(from:), delegating to new Context methods over
llama_state_save_file / llama_state_load_file.

The one subtlety: restoring the raw KV cache alone is NOT safe. Context tracks its own prompt
cache bookkeeping (promptCaches), which drives prefix reuse in removeCachedChunks(). Restoring
KV while that array stayed empty would leave the context believing nothing is cached even though
positions are occupied, so the next generation would append instead of reuse and corrupt the
conversation. saveState therefore writes a small sidecar (<url>.meta) with the bookkeeping, and
loadState restores both together; a state file whose sidecar is missing is refused rather than
loaded into an inconsistent context.

Multimodal sessions are out of scope for now: image/video chunks are not serialisable here, so
saveState throws invalidParameter instead of silently dropping them.

Tests (ContextStateTests, run against a real model): a decoded context saves and restores into
a fresh client with the same position; a missing state file throws; a state file with its
sidecar removed throws.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
